WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:10.100
Voyager 2 started in 1977. It flew near
Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune.

2
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:15.306
At each planet, the flight path turned and
the speed changed. How did this occur?

3
00:00:15.706 --> 00:00:20.784
Look at the flyby of Jupiter in
July 1979, relative to Jupiter.

4
00:00:20.824 --> 00:00:24.880
Relative to Jupiter, the
spacecraft moves on a hyperbola.

5
00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:30.640
It comes in at 7.64 kilometers per
second. It goes out at the same speed.

6
00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:36.379
Only the direction changes. Here,
it turns through 97.6 degrees.

7
00:00:36.779 --> 00:00:40.148
A flyby nearer to the planet gives a larger turn.

8
00:00:40.188 --> 00:00:47.905
This curve shows the turn angle for each distance of
closest approach. Voyager 2 went to 10 radii of Jupiter.

9
00:00:48.505 --> 00:00:51.533
Now look at the same flyby relative to the Sun.

10
00:00:51.573 --> 00:01:00.072
Jupiter moves at 12.7 kilometers per second. Add the
velocity of Jupiter to the two velocities of the spacecraft.

11
00:01:00.272 --> 00:01:09.292
Before the flyby, the speed relative to the Sun is 9.6
kilometers per second. After the flyby, it is 19.5.

12
00:01:09.492 --> 00:01:16.359
The spacecraft gets speed from the motion of Jupiter.
The speed of Jupiter decreases by a very small quantity.

13
00:01:16.399 --> 00:01:19.916
This is a gravity assist. It uses no propellant.

14
00:01:20.516 --> 00:01:25.262
Here is the full flight of Voyager 2,
with its speed relative to the Sun.

15
00:01:25.302 --> 00:01:30.227
The dashed line is the escape speed
from the solar system at each distance.

16
00:01:30.267 --> 00:01:34.246
After Jupiter, Voyager 2 was
faster than the escape speed.

17
00:01:34.286 --> 00:01:45.283
At Neptune, the flyby decreased the speed from 18.9 to 16.7 kilometers
per second. But the spacecraft stayed faster than the escape speed.

18
00:01:45.883 --> 00:01:52.363
Apsis calculates this flight with 3 small
corrections, a total of 104 meters per second.

19
00:01:52.403 --> 00:02:04.276
The distances of closest approach agree with JPL Horizons within
1.02 percent. The speeds at infinity agree within 0.45 percent.

20
00:02:04.677 --> 00:02:09.320
In the Expert mode, use the Flyby tour
screen to design flights of this type.

21
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.049
In the Explorer mode, you
can fly the actual missions.
